In this video, we are going to learn about kafka topic and partitions.


1. topic:

a) Named container for similar events. Unique identifier of a topic is its name.

b) Example: Student topic will have student related data, Food Topic will have food related data.

c) They live inside a broker.

d) Producer produce a message ito the topic(ultimately to partitions in round robin fashion) or directly to the partitions. Consumer poll continuously for new messages using the topic name.

e) Partition - A topic is partitioned and distributed to kafka brokers in round robin fashion to achieve distributed system.

f) Replication factor - A partition is replicated by this factor and it is replicated in another broker to prevent fault tolerance.



2. Partitions:

a) A topic is split into several parts which are known as the partitions of the topic.

b) Partitions is where actually the message is located inside the topic.

c) Therefore, while creating a topic, we need to specify the number of partitions ( the number is arbitrary and can be changed later).

d) Each partition is an ordered, immutable sequence of records.

e) Each partition is independent of each other.

f) Each message gets stored into partitions with an incremental id known as its Offset value.

g) Ordering is there only at partition level. (So if data is to be stored in order then do it on same partition.)

h) Partition continuosly grows(offset increases) as new records are produced.

i) All the records exist in distributed log file.
